  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1250 PM EST Fri Nov 13 2026\n\nCurrently a deep mid/upper trough is over the eastern CONUS. Low \nlevel flow is from the northwest over our area with cold advection \nongoing. A lobe of short wave energy is moving through the trough \nand is currently moving southeast an KY and is will swing across \nlater today, with another wave not far behind currently approaching \nthe Ohio Valley.  The first of the two short waves will enhance rain \nshowers/high elevations snow showers this afternoon, with another \nuptick in activity expected with the second wave early tonight. NW \nflow around 15 to 25 kts will bring an orographic boost to the \nnormally favored upslope areas. Light snow has already accumulated \nover the highest peaks and freezing levels will continue to drop \nfurther later today into tonight. Additional accumulating snow will \noccur over the higher elevations later this afternoon and tonight \nespecially above 3000 ft, and a few flurries or a light snow shower \nmay even make it to the ground over some valley locations overnight. \nThe winter weather advisory will remain if effect for elevations \nabove 3000 feet in the E TN mountains, but lighter snow \naccumulations are likely across higher elevation areas in SW VA as \nwell.  An SPS will be issued for these lighter amounts. The \nprecipitation will taper off later tonight into Saturday as the \nmoisture becomes more shallow and the upslope flow weakens, but it \nwill remain cold.  \n\nThe upper trough will begin shifting off to the east Sunday, and \nridging will gradually build in for next week. It looks like we will \nsee another extended dry period for Sunday through next Friday. \nSunday will still be on the chilly side, but we will see a gradual \nwarming trend for next week.",
